  two module systems that don't currently meet.

  [1mThe fork:[0m whether adding logging also migrates this repo's module system.

  - [1mSingle dual-mode file ([38;5;153mlogger.js[39m)[0m — defines the logger, attaches it to [38;5;153mglobalThis[39m for the browser script, and does
    [38;5;153mmodule.exports[39m when [38;5;153mmodule[39m exists for Node. Works in both with no build step, no [38;5;153mpackage.json[39m change, no edits to
    [38;5;153mindex.html[39m or [38;5;153mutils.js[39m. Slightly dated pattern, but it's honest about what this repo is. Easy to replace with ESM
    later.
  - [1mConvert everything to ESM[0m — [38;5;153m"type": "module"[39m in [38;5;153mpackage.json[39m, [38;5;153m<script type="module">[39m in [38;5;153mindex.html[39m, [38;5;153mimport[39m/[38;5;153mexport[39m in
    all four files. Cleaner long-term and where JS has landed. But it changes [38;5;153mutils.js[39m and [38;5;153mindex.js[39m for reasons
    unrelated to logging, and a bare [38;5;153mtype="module"[39m script won't run from [38;5;153mfile://[39m — it needs a server, which changes how
    you open this app.
  - [1mSeparate browser and Node builds from one core[0m — a core file plus two shims. No benefit at four files and ~60 lines.

  [1mMy recommendation: the single dual-mode file.[0m It's the only option that adds logging without dragging a module-system
  migration along, and your CLAUDE.md is explicit about not bundling unrelated refactors. If you want ESM, that's a
  worthwhile separate change with its own approval.
